+
+.. _hookimpl_arg_default:
+
+Hookimpl argument defaults
+^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^
+A *hookimpl* may also declare a default value for an argument. The value the
+*hookimpl* receives is, in order of precedence:
+
+1. the value passed by the hook call,
+2. the *hookspec*'s default value for the argument,
+3. the *hookimpl*'s own default value.
+
+The *hookimpl*'s default is therefore only used when neither the call nor the
+*hookspec* provides the argument, for example when a new *hookimpl* runs
+against an older *hookspec* which doesn't have the argument yet:
+
+.. code-block:: python
+
+    @hookspec
+    def myhook(config, args):
+        pass
+
+
+    @hookimpl
+    def myhook(config, args, new_arg="fallback"):
+        # new_arg is "fallback" until the hookspec gains ``new_arg``.
+        print(new_arg)
+
+This applies to plain *hookimpls* and to both new-style and old-style wrappers.
+
+.. versionchanged:: 1.7
+    Previously the *hookimpl*'s default was always used, even when the hook
+    call passed a value for the argument.
 
